  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n\nThe current approach relies on local gradient-based optimization (SLSQP and Nesterov subgradient) which often gets trapped in local optima for non-convex problems. The new strategy adopts a population-based evolution strategy that balances global exploration and local refinement. \n\nKey improvements:\n- Uses covariance matrix adaptation evolution strategy (CMA-ES) to navigate complex fitness landscapes\n- Incorporates dimensionality reduction through level-set parameterization\n- Maintains adaptive learning rates and uses pairwise comparisons between samples\n- Includes dimension doubling when promising high-performance regions are detected\n- Implements explicit restarts from promising regions and multi-resolution transfers\n\nThe central idea is that CMA-ES provides better global exploration while still allowing for fine optimization steps. This addresses the bottleneck of local optimization by combining broad search with adaptive refinement.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n\n- Decision variables:\n  - **x**: Level-set parameter controlling h(x) transitions\n  - **\u03b8**: Local optimization parameter\n  - **\u03b3**: Dimension adaptivity factor\n\n- Objective optimization:\n  - Minimize C\u2085 = max\u2096 \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n  - Overlapped integral approximation using full-mode correlation multiplied by dx\n\n- Level-set parameterization:\n  - h(x) = 1 if x < x\u2080 or x > 1-x\u2080 (after normalization)\n  - x\u2080 parameterized by a few samples and quadratic interpolation\n\nConstraints:\nforce in [0,1] \u2200x\n\u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)dx = 1\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n\n  1. Setup Problem Features\n      - Set target sum S = n/2.0\n      - Compute initial solution if initial_h_values is available (project to [0,1] space)\n      - Initialize hyperparameters: \u03bc=100, \u03c3=1.0, \u03bb=50, c\u2081=1, c\u2082=2, \u03c1=0.5, etc.\n\n  2. Covariance Matrix Adaptation\n      - Initialize covariance matrix C\u2080 with uniform scaling\n      - For each generation:\n        * Generate \u03bb samples from Gaussian random vector m\u209c with covariance \u03c3\u00b2C\u209c\n        * Evaluate fitness for each sample\n        * Select best \u03bc samples and update:\n          m\u209c\u208a\u00b9 = (1-\u03c1)m\u209c + \u03c1 x\u0304\n          C\u209c\u208a\u00b9 = (1-c\u2081-c\u2082)C\u209c + c\u2081*C\u2081 + c\u2082*C\u2082\n        * Scale samples by \u03c3 and \u03c3/\u221a(1+\u03c3\u00b2) respectively\n\n  3. Level-set Construction\n      - If \u03c3 < threshold, double variable space dimension\n      - Initialize new samples:\n        * Sample from normal distribution with covariance parameters\n        * Project to feasible solutions\n        * Add midpoints between best individuals\n\n  4. Local Refinement Steps\n      - Apply SLSQP algorithm to best samples after \u03bc evaluations\n      - After successful refinement, double population size\n      - Maintain a restart mechanism from periodically selected individuals\n\n  5. Feasibility Assessment\n      - After each generation, clip solutions to [0,1]\n      - Ensure solution has sum closest to S\n      - Calculate C\u2085 value using projected feasible points\n\n  6. Termination Criteria\n      - Time budget expires or C\u2085 improvement plateaus (no improvement in 10 generations)\n      - Store best solution at 50% completion mark\n      - Return best candidate with lowest C\u2085\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n\n  Primary implementation with adaptive covariance matrix:\n    - Initial \u03bc=50, \u03c3=0.5, \u03bb=100\n    - Adjustment frequency: 50 fitness evaluations\n    - Dimensionality boost at C\u2085 improvements <0.0001\n\n  Variations applied when performance stalls:\n    Variation A (Random Restarts):\n      - Evaluate new sample from uniform [0,1] space\n      - Restore feasibility and check fitness\n    Variation B (Gradient Informed):\n      - Use finite differences to estimate gradient\n      - Apply gradient descent step for one iteration\n    Variation C (Dimension Boost):\n      - If C\u2085 has improved in rare events, double variable space\n      - Evaluate new solutions in expanded space\n\n  Each variation triggers after a plateau of 3 distinct individuals\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n\n  Dominant costs:\n    - Objective computation: O(n\u00b2) per evaluation\n    - Correlation matrix storage: O(n) memory\n\n  Time estimation:\n    - Level-set evaluation: O(n\u00b7log(n))\n    - Gradient-based refinement: O(n\u00b7n) operations\n    - Data storage: O(\u03bc\u00b7n) per generation\n\n  Monitoring and emergency protocols:\n    - Check runtime budgets at each dimension increase\n    - Return best candidate at 70% budget utilization\n    - Store entire covariance matrix for restarts\n    - Implement automatic dimension reduction if memory issues\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n\n  Feasibility checks:\n    - Confirm \u2211h = n/2 (absolute tolerance 1e-4)\n    - Ensure all h[i]\u2208[0,1]\n    - Compute C\u2085 via signed correlation differences\n\n  Objective re-estimation:\n    - Use same correlation with dx scaling\n    - Validate using small-size examples (n=20)\n    - Calculate finite-difference gradient if requested\n\n  Output preparation:\n    - Store best solution at 50% time\n    - Normalize final h_values if n changed\n    - Project final solution back to original space",
